{"parentUuid":"00e50cc1-898c-46a8-b3c4-f40d7502963a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgbGyu5NPGchosc2vYAaM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F8CeECLdfyPmTmKeTrSCUf","name":"mcp__devrelay-gateway__get_challenge_details","input":{"id":82},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":692,"cache_read_input_tokens":594656,"output_tokens":64,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":692,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":64,"cache_read_input_tokens":594656,"cache_creation_input_tokens":692,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":692},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01F8CeECLdfyPmTmKeTrSCUf":{"id":82}},"serverClassifierRequest":"8c30f579-3918-40d3-80af-d1a58668654c","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgbGytepfFc62vnuYHWkz","requestRef":"5ea9a467-8e24-4659-88a2-b19220d57923","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"bacd65b7-c3aa-4c61-8635-bc0183e1d704","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:39:40.450Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5be3b0-ab69-4299-a8b5-d7494c65687b","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"submission","slug":"sunny-popping-comet"}
{"parentUuid":"bacd65b7-c3aa-4c61-8635-bc0183e1d704","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6a3b8f65-5b37-4be0-a62e-48e785b5e2f9","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F8CeECLdfyPmTmKeTrSCUf","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"Ok\":{\"id\":82,\"title\":\"Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge: Week 4\",\"slug\":\"hacktoberfest-week4-2026-10-26\",\"description\":\"The Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge: Week 4 runs October 26 to October 31. Build something spooky, or something that honors the past in the spirit of Día de los Muertos, with open-weight models or open-source AI. $2,850 in prizes across 21 winners.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is the final challenge of our Hacktoberfest series. This year's Hacktoberfest is all about building brand-new projects with open-source AI and open-weight models, and the theme is intentionally broad, so interpret it however you'd like!\",\"details\":null,\"full_details\":\"The Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge: Week 4 runs October 26 to October 31. Build something spooky, or something that honors the past in the spirit of Día de los Muertos, with open-weight models or open-source AI. $2,850 in prizes across 21 winners.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is the final challenge of our Hacktoberfest series. This year's Hacktoberfest is all about building brand-new projects with open-source AI and open-weight models, and the theme is intentionally broad, so interpret it however you'd like!\\r\\n\\r\\nOur Theme: Ghost in the Machine\\r\\n\\r\\nBuild something spooky, or something that honors the past in the spirit of Día de los Muertos, with open-weight models or open-source AI.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis week brings us Halloween on October 31 and Día de los Muertos on November 1 and 2, a stretch of the calendar when the line between past and present feels a little thinner. Whether that brings to mind things that go bump in the night, ofrendas glowing with marigolds and candlelight, or the memories and stories we carry with us, we want to see what it inspires you to build.\\r\\n\\r\\nThat can mean running an open-weight model, building on an open-source agent harness or framework, running inference locally, or all three. Whatever you pick, the open pieces should be what makes your project work.\\r\\n\\r\\nA few ideas to get you going:\\r\\n\\r\\n    A haunted terminal that \\\"possesses\\\" your shell and whispers back eerie commentary on the commands you run\\r\\n    A digital ofrenda builder that helps you write a remembrance for a loved one and gather their favorite foods, songs, and stories\\r\\n    An offline séance chatbot that channels the spirit of a long-dead programming language\\r\\n    A local oral history tool that records and transcribes family stories so they're never lost\\r\\n    A ghost story generator that weaves your smart home's sensor logs into a creepy tale of what happened while you were asleep\\r\\n    A project that brings an abandoned open-source tool or a forgotten corner of internet history back to life\\r\\n\\r\\nIn your post, tell us why open innovation matters for what you built. Does running your own model mean your project works with the lights (and the internet) off? Keep your family's photos and stories private and on your own machine? Make sure what you build isn't locked inside a service that might disappear someday? Tell us where your open-based approach worked better than a closed one.\\r\\n\\r\\nPrize Categories\\r\\n\\r\\nAlongside our overall winner, we have 20 prize categories for projects that use a specific partner's technology. You don't need to use any of these to win the overall prize. Full descriptions are on the HF26 DEV Challenge Hub (https://dev.to/challenges/hf26).\\r\\n\\r\\nFeatured categories: Best Use of Render, Best Use of TabPFN, Best Use of Tinker, Best Use of Arduino, Best Use of DigitalOcean, Best Use of Gemma\\r\\n\\r\\nPartner categories: Best Use of Auth0 for AI Agents, Best Use of Backboard, Best Use of Browserbase, Best Use of ElevenLabs, Best Use of Entire, Best Use of GitHub Copilot, Best Use of Mastra, Best Use of MongoDB Atlas, Best Use of Sentry Agent Tracing, Best Use of SerpApi, Best Use of Temporal, Best Use of Tiger Data, Best Use of Ultramarine (Paper Instruments), Best Use of a .tech Domain\\r\\n\\r\\nOne project can enter every category it genuinely uses, but you can only win once per challenge. Several partners are giving participants free credits and promo codes at hacktoberfest.com/my.\\r\\n\\r\\nJudging Criteria\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is DEV, so your write-up matters most. Submissions will be evaluated for:\\r\\n\\r\\n    Writing Quality (weighted most heavily)\\r\\n    Relevance to the Prompt and Theme\\r\\n    Creativity\\r\\n    Technical Execution\\r\\n    Use of Partner Technology (optional, for prize category entries)\\r\\n\\r\\nShow your work: saving your agent session with DevRelay and embedding it in your post is optional, but it helps the judges understand your process.\\r\\n\\r\\nPrizes 🏆\\r\\n\\r\\nOverall winner (1) will receive:\\r\\n\\r\\n    $250 USD cash prize\\r\\n    DEV++ Membership\\r\\n    Exclusive DEV Badge\\r\\n\\r\\nFeatured category winners (6) will each receive:\\r\\n\\r\\n    $200 USD cash prize\\r\\n    Exclusive DEV Badge\\r\\n\\r\\nPartner category winners (14) will each receive:\\r\\n\\r\\n    $100 USD cash prize\\r\\n    Exclusive DEV Badge\\r\\n\\r\\nAll participants with a valid submission will receive a completion badge on their DEV profile.\\r\\n\\r\\nHow To Participate\\r\\n\\r\\nPublish a post on DEV using the submission template on the challenge page and be sure to include the required challenge tag: #hf26challenge. Tell us what you built and how you interpreted the theme, show us a demo, and explain why open innovation matters for what you built.\\r\\n\\r\\nIf your app requires logging in, please provide testing credentials in your submission and/or instructions on how to best test your application for judges.\\r\\n\\r\\nImportant Dates\\r\\n\\r\\n    October 26: Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge: Week 4 begins!\\r\\n    October 31: Submissions due at 11:59 PM PDT\\r\\n    Week of November 2: Winners Announced\",\"type_of\":\"challenge\",\"starts_at\":\"2026-10-26T19:00:00.000Z\",\"ends_at\":\"2026-10-31T06:59:00.000Z\",\"published\":true,\"cover_image\":\"https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/events/cover_image/82/e4131c99-f797-42be-95ef-b55e1f04255f.png\",\"location_url\":null}}"}]}]},"uuid":"94b03ff7-b821-41c8-92dd-7ce696add0b3","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:39:42.352Z","toolUseResult":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"Ok\":{\"id\":82,\"title\":\"Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge: Week 4\",\"slug\":\"hacktoberfest-week4-2026-10-26\",\"description\":\"The Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge: Week 4 runs October 26 to October 31. Build something spooky, or something that honors the past in the spirit of Día de los Muertos, with open-weight models or open-source AI. $2,850 in prizes across 21 winners.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is the final challenge of our Hacktoberfest series. This year's Hacktoberfest is all about building brand-new projects with open-source AI and open-weight models, and the theme is intentionally broad, so interpret it however you'd like!\",\"details\":null,\"full_details\":\"The Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge: Week 4 runs October 26 to October 31. Build something spooky, or something that honors the past in the spirit of Día de los Muertos, with open-weight models or open-source AI. $2,850 in prizes across 21 winners.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is the final challenge of our Hacktoberfest series. This year's Hacktoberfest is all about building brand-new projects with open-source AI and open-weight models, and the theme is intentionally broad, so interpret it however you'd like!\\r\\n\\r\\nOur Theme: Ghost in the Machine\\r\\n\\r\\nBuild something spooky, or something that honors the past in the spirit of Día de los Muertos, with open-weight models or open-source AI.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis week brings us Halloween on October 31 and Día de los Muertos on November 1 and 2, a stretch of the calendar when the line between past and present feels a little thinner. Whether that brings to mind things that go bump in the night, ofrendas glowing with marigolds and candlelight, or the memories and stories we carry with us, we want to see what it inspires you to build.\\r\\n\\r\\nThat can mean running an open-weight model, building on an open-source agent harness or framework, running inference locally, or all three. Whatever you pick, the open pieces should be what makes your project work.\\r\\n\\r\\nA few ideas to get you going:\\r\\n\\r\\n    A haunted terminal that \\\"possesses\\\" your shell and whispers back eerie commentary on the commands you run\\r\\n    A digital ofrenda builder that helps you write a remembrance for a loved one and gather their favorite foods, songs, and stories\\r\\n    An offline séance chatbot that channels the spirit of a long-dead programming language\\r\\n    A local oral history tool that records and transcribes family stories so they're never lost\\r\\n    A ghost story generator that weaves your smart home's sensor logs into a creepy tale of what happened while you were asleep\\r\\n    A project that brings an abandoned open-source tool or a forgotten corner of internet history back to life\\r\\n\\r\\nIn your post, tell us why open innovation matters for what you built. Does running your own model mean your project works with the lights (and the internet) off? Keep your family's photos and stories private and on your own machine? Make sure what you build isn't locked inside a service that might disappear someday? Tell us where your open-based approach worked better than a closed one.\\r\\n\\r\\nPrize Categories\\r\\n\\r\\nAlongside our overall winner, we have 20 prize categories for projects that use a specific partner's technology. You don't need to use any of these to win the overall prize. Full descriptions are on the HF26 DEV Challenge Hub (https://dev.to/challenges/hf26).\\r\\n\\r\\nFeatured categories: Best Use of Render, Best Use of TabPFN, Best Use of Tinker, Best Use of Arduino, Best Use of DigitalOcean, Best Use of Gemma\\r\\n\\r\\nPartner categories: Best Use of Auth0 for AI Agents, Best Use of Backboard, Best Use of Browserbase, Best Use of ElevenLabs, Best Use of Entire, Best Use of GitHub Copilot, Best Use of Mastra, Best Use of MongoDB Atlas, Best Use of Sentry Agent Tracing, Best Use of SerpApi, Best Use of Temporal, Best Use of Tiger Data, Best Use of Ultramarine (Paper Instruments), Best Use of a .tech Domain\\r\\n\\r\\nOne project can enter every category it genuinely uses, but you can only win once per challenge. Several partners are giving participants free credits and promo codes at hacktoberfest.com/my.\\r\\n\\r\\nJudging Criteria\\r\\n\\r\\nThis is DEV, so your write-up matters most. Submissions will be evaluated for:\\r\\n\\r\\n    Writing Quality (weighted most heavily)\\r\\n    Relevance to the Prompt and Theme\\r\\n    Creativity\\r\\n    Technical Execution\\r\\n    Use of Partner Technology (optional, for prize category entries)\\r\\n\\r\\nShow your work: saving your agent session with DevRelay and embedding it in your post is optional, but it helps the judges understand your process.\\r\\n\\r\\nPrizes 🏆\\r\\n\\r\\nOverall winner (1) will receive:\\r\\n\\r\\n    $250 USD cash prize\\r\\n    DEV++ Membership\\r\\n    Exclusive DEV Badge\\r\\n\\r\\nFeatured category winners (6) will each receive:\\r\\n\\r\\n    $200 USD cash prize\\r\\n    Exclusive DEV Badge\\r\\n\\r\\nPartner category winners (14) will each receive:\\r\\n\\r\\n    $100 USD cash prize\\r\\n    Exclusive DEV Badge\\r\\n\\r\\nAll participants with a valid submission will receive a completion badge on their DEV profile.\\r\\n\\r\\nHow To Participate\\r\\n\\r\\nPublish a post on DEV using the submission template on the challenge page and be sure to include the required challenge tag: #hf26challenge. Tell us what you built and how you interpreted the theme, show us a demo, and explain why open innovation matters for what you built.\\r\\n\\r\\nIf your app requires logging in, please provide testing credentials in your submission and/or instructions on how to best test your application for judges.\\r\\n\\r\\nImportant Dates\\r\\n\\r\\n    October 26: Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge: Week 4 begins!\\r\\n    October 31: Submissions due at 11:59 PM PDT\\r\\n    Week of November 2: Winners Announced\",\"type_of\":\"challenge\",\"starts_at\":\"2026-10-26T19:00:00.000Z\",\"ends_at\":\"2026-10-31T06:59:00.000Z\",\"published\":true,\"cover_image\":\"https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/events/cover_image/82/e4131c99-f797-42be-95ef-b55e1f04255f.png\",\"location_url\":null}}"}],"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"8c30f579-3918-40d3-80af-d1a58668654c","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","root":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","branch":"submission","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Hotragn/Paperghosts","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bacd65b7-c3aa-4c61-8635-bc0183e1d704","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts","sessionId":"1f5