{"parentUuid":"3c7d5a9f-0982-43ba-a700-05700562c935","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgbHv4vTgXCDjoj9sS9qm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TZnFLAmCoUTjMCCD1mKjM9","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd \"/Users/hotragn/Documents/Claude Code/HacktoberFest/week4/Paperghosts\" && python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"Docs/POST.md\"); s = p.read_text()\nold = \"The teacher's dev answers were produced by a local model through Ollama, and nothing about the archive left the machine to make them.\"\nassert old in s\ns = s.replace(old, \"gemma4's dev answers were produced on this laptop through Ollama, and nothing about the archive left the machine to make them.\")\nanchor = \"That's a ghost in the machine, in 1879: a projector making a haunting.\"\ni = s.index(anchor); j = s.index(\"\\n\\n\", i)\ns = s[:j] + \"\"\"\n\nHalloween is half of this week's theme. Día de los Muertos is the other half, and it suits the Morgue better, because these reports are about real people, named by their own papers. They include Detective John Burnside, who caught the Springfield gang, and Miss Parsons, the parish clerk's daughter at Cock Lane, twelve years old, who heard the knocking. The Morgue remembers them in the words that were printed about them, and adds nothing.\"\"\" + s[j:]\np.write_text(s)\nn = Path(\"scripts/article_numbers.py\"); t = n.read_text()\nold = '''        *sentry(),'''\nassert old in t\nt = t.replace(old, '''        *sentry(),\n        row(\n            \"Entire: the reprint chaining fix session, tokens and API calls\",\n            doc(\"Docs/demo/entire_sessions.md\", r\"`01M67W8SMF2WGS6DNG76TSEAXP`\\\\): ([\\\\d,]+) tokens over (\\\\d+) API calls\"),\n        ),\n        row(\n            \"Entire: the blind re-ruling session, tokens and API calls\",\n            doc(\"Docs/demo/entire_sessions.md\", r\"`01M69PCNVRXPX8DD1EWAHB57JF`\\\\): ([\\\\d,]+) tokens over (\\\\